{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ומצת", :translit "u'matzot", :gloss "and matzot/unleavened bread"} {:he "ב' במסורה", :translit "bet b'masorah", :gloss "two in the Masorah"} {:he "צלי אש ומצות", :translit "tzli eish u'matzot", :gloss "roasted fire and unleavened bread"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["ומצת" "ומצות"]}], :frame {:text "The word ומצת appears twice in Scripture: here in Genesis 19:3 and in Numbers 9:11 in the phrase 'roasted with fire and unleavened bread.' The Numbers passage explicitly describes the Paschal offering, so the shared rare word links the Genesis narrative to Passover observance. The hermeneutic of learning one passage from another via shared terminology is the standard gezeirah shavah method (see Sifra, introduction to Vayikra, listing gezeirah shavah among the thirteen middot). The specific derivation that Abraham's guests ate matzah because it was Passover appears in Bereishit Rabbah 48:12 and is cited by Rashi on Genesis 19:3.", :sources ["Genesis 19:3" "Numbers 9:11" "Bereishit Rabbah 48:12" "Rashi on Genesis 19:3"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["ומצת" "ומצות"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "ומצת", :refs ["Genesis 19:3" "Genesis 26:5" "Exodus 12:8" "Exodus 12:15" "Exodus 12:18" "Exodus 12:20" "Exodus 12:39" "Exodus 13:6"], :n 130, :lens :plene-insensitive} {:phrase "ומצות", :refs ["Genesis 19:3" "Exodus 12:8" "Nehemiah 9:13"], :n 3, :lens :exact}]}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:06:43.838338055Z", :model "glm-5.2"}